WHAT: make the app a wk node -- default to the `wk` QPA platform, statically
      import the three plugins wasm cannot dlopen, link libqwk.a and the
      component-type object, and compile a font in as a Qt resource.
WHY:  four things a wasm32-wasip2 Qt node needs that a desktop build gets for
      free:
        * QT_QPA_PLATFORM. plugins/qt's qtbase was configured
          QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen (it predates the wk QPA plugin), so
          an unmodified app renders to nowhere and never opens a surface. Set
          only when the environment does not already say, so
          QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen stays available for bisecting failures.
        * Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN. No dlopen on wasm. Without QWkIntegrationPlugin,
          QApplication aborts with "no Qt platform plugin could be initialized"
          and an EMPTY plugin list -- it reads like a broken Qt, but it is an
          unreferenced symbol. QSvgPlugin/QSvgIconPlugin are what let
          aboutdlg.ui's <pixmap>:/icons/app.svg and the window icon decode;
          upstream links Qt6::Svg but, being a shared-Qt build, never names the
          plugins.
        * the component-type object must arrive as a LINK OPTION. It holds only
          a `component-type` custom section that wasm-component-ld reads to
          build the import list; nothing references it, so as an archive member
          it is dropped and the binary ends up with no wasi:surface import.
        * fonts. Qt 6 ships none and a wk node has no host font dir; with none
          the app runs, QFontDatabase is empty, and every string renders as
          nothing at all.
      All four are guarded (`#ifdef __wasi__`, `if(WK_...)`) so a desktop build
      of this tree is byte-for-byte unaffected.
UPSTREAM: as-is in spirit -- this is the standard static-Qt deployment shape,
      the same thing upstream already does for its MXE static Windows build
      (main.cpp's existing Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN block). It is wk-specific only in
      naming OUR platform plugin.
--- a/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-26 02:23:33
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-26 02:23:20
@@ -353,6 +353,19 @@
   resources.qrc
 )
 
+# --- wk node: fonts ---------------------------------------------------------
+# Qt 6 ships no fonts and a wk node has no host font directory to fall back on:
+# with none, the app runs, QFontDatabase is empty and every string renders as
+# nothing at all. build.sh stages one TTF and generates this .qrc beside it
+# (which font exists depends on the machine), and the wk QPA plugin's font
+# database falls back to :/fonts after looking in QT_QPA_FONTDIR and the usual
+# VFS directories. A node that bind-mounts a real font directory can still set
+# QT_QPA_FONTDIR and never touch this copy.
+set(WK_FONTS_QRC "" CACHE FILEPATH "generated .qrc holding one staged TTF under :/fonts")
+if(WK_FONTS_QRC)
+  list(APPEND RESOURCES "${WK_FONTS_QRC}")
+endif()
+
 # The lupdate/fix_* maintainer targets are gone with LinguistTools: every one
 # of them names the Qt6::lupdate imported target, which does not exist without
 # find_package(Qt6LinguistTools), so merely DECLARING them is a configure
@@ -436,6 +449,43 @@
 
 if(APPLE)
   target_link_libraries(${EXE_NAME} ${SPARKLE_LIBRARY} "-framework AppKit")
+endif()
+
+# --- wk node: the platform plugin and the component's imports ---------------
+# There is no dlopen on wasm, so every Qt plugin is an ARCHIVE linked into the
+# binary and named once with Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN (see main.cpp).
+#
+#   WK_QPA_LIB            plugins/qt/sysroot/lib/libqwk.a -- the wk QPA plugin.
+#                         It also carries the gfx-compat shim objects
+#                         (wkgfx.c + the wit-bindgen output), so the app needs
+#                         no other wasi-gfx code.
+#   WK_SVG_PLUGINS        the qsvg imageformat + iconengine plugins. Without
+#                         them Qt6::Svg is linked but no QImageReader knows
+#                         what an .svg is, and aboutdlg.ui's <pixmap>
+#                         :/icons/app.svg (and the window icon) come out null.
+#   WK_GFX_COMPONENT_TYPE gen/wkgfx_component_type.o, passed as a LINK OPTION
+#                         and never as a library. It carries only a
+#                         `component-type` custom section that
+#                         wasm-component-ld reads to build the component's
+#                         import list; nothing references it, so as an archive
+#                         member the linker drops it and the result is a
+#                         component with NO wasi:surface import at all.
+set(WK_QPA_LIB "" CACHE FILEPATH "libqwk.a, the wk QPA plugin (plugins/qt/build-qpa.sh)")
+set(WK_SVG_PLUGINS "" CACHE STRING "static qsvg plugin archives")
+set(WK_GFX_COMPONENT_TYPE "" CACHE FILEPATH "gfx-compat gen/wkgfx_component_type.o")
+if(WK_QPA_LIB)
+  # FbSupportPrivate is not optional and not obvious: the wk QPA plugin IS
+  # fbconvenience -- QWkScreen derives from QFbScreen, QWkWindow from
+  # QFbWindow, QWkBackingStore from QFbBackingStore, and that is where the
+  # guest-side compositing of N top-level windows into one surface lives.
+  # Without it the link fails with a page of "undefined symbol: QFbScreen::..."
+  # from inside libqwk.a. It is an INTERNAL_MODULE, so it is its own package
+  # rather than a component of Qt6Gui.
+  find_package(Qt6 6.8 REQUIRED COMPONENTS FbSupportPrivate)
+  target_link_libraries(${EXE_NAME} ${WK_SVG_PLUGINS} "${WK_QPA_LIB}" Qt6::FbSupportPrivate)
+endif()
+if(WK_GFX_COMPONENT_TYPE)
+  target_link_options(${EXE_NAME} PRIVATE "${WK_GFX_COMPONENT_TYPE}")
 endif()
 
 
